---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Single-location dental practice, Veneto' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Initiative in 2026, part of an ongoing relationship since 2023' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Strategic discovery, copywriting, post-lead sales protocol, campaign management, reporting' },
  { label: 'Channel', value: 'Meta Ads' },
  { label: 'Team', value: 'Practice owner (clinical input), front desk (protocol execution)' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '8.53×', label: 'ROAS on ad spend' },
  { metric: '83.3%', label: 'close rate after the visit was completed' },
  { metric: '23.8%', label: 'from contact to closed treatment' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Building a specialist positioning, from discovery to closing the quote"
  description="Case study: clinical-communication positioning for a complex service, with discovery, copywriting and a sales protocol. 8.53x ROAS."
  eyebrow="Strategy · Copywriting · Funnel"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2 class="fade-in">The context</h2>
  <p>
    Periodontitis is serious, widespread, and almost invisible to the people who have it — it
    doesn't hurt at first, and the warning signs get written off again and again. For most
    practices, it's easier to sell an implant than to save the tooth. The owner wanted the
    opposite: to become the go-to name in the area for patients who'd rather save theirs.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Make a condition the patient can't feel seem real and urgent — without alarmism, and within
    the rules on healthcare communication. Then build a post-lead journey that turns clinical
    understanding into an actual treatment decision.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">My role</h2>
  <p>
    I ran the discovery with the owner myself: a structured interview to pull out the target
    audience, the clinical signals patients overlook, the objections that keep coming up, the tone
    of voice. From that I built the communication framework — six distinct angles, from "35 and
    already being offered implants?" to a straight cost comparison between treatment and implant —
    and wrote all eight campaign copy variants. Then, together with the front desk, I defined the
    post-lead protocol: what to say on the phone, what has to happen at every first visit, how to
    present the treatment plan.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    The funnel showed its winners and losers immediately. Almost one contact in two books a visit
    — 47.6% — and whoever makes it into the practice closes treatment 83.3% of the time. The weak
    link sits in the middle: only 60% of booked visits actually show up. The problem was never
    lead quality. It was what happened in the room.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    Open point: we've since tightened front-desk follow-up to improve the show-up rate, but
    there's no fresh data yet to prove it worked. Better to say that plainly than claim a win I
    can't back up.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Execution</h2>
  <p>
    Eight copy variants within one consistent creative territory, tested live on Meta. A phone
    script for the front desk built to lead with value, not price. A standardized first visit —
    exams, photo documentation, clinical record — designed to let patients actually see their own
    situation, turning the diagnosis into a moment of awareness instead of a quote dropped on
    them.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Results</h2>
  <p>
    42 contacts produced 20 booked visits, 12 shown, 10 closed treatments — €18,845 in accepted
    value against €2,208.34 in ad spend, a ROAS of 8.53×. Almost one contact in four, 23.8%, made
    it all the way to closed treatment.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    A complex clinical insight can become a repeatable communication framework — if the discovery
    behind it is done properly. And the post-lead funnel has to be built with whoever runs it
    every day. The most effective script isn't the cleverest one; it's the one the front desk
    actually makes their own.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
